      'jpcal.title': '📅 Japanese calendar', 'jpcal.subtitle': 'Events overlapping your trip dates',
      'jpcal.gw.label': 'Golden Week', 'jpcal.gw.msg': 'Week of multiple public holidays (Showa Day, Constitution Day, Greenery Day, Children\'s Day). Hotels full, prices 2-3× average, Shinkansen sold out, famous attractions with huge lines. Not recommended for iconic sightseeing (Kyoto, Disney, Universal). If unavoidable, book 6 months ahead.',
      'jpcal.obon.label': 'Obon', 'jpcal.obon.msg': 'Festival of the dead: whole families travel back to their hometowns. Shinkansen packed, hotels pricey, restaurants often closed in tourist areas. On the plus side, cities are quieter — can be an opportunity if you avoid intercity routes.',
      'jpcal.shogatsu.label': 'New Year (Shogatsu)', 'jpcal.shogatsu.msg': 'Japanese New Year: the most important holiday. Almost everything closed on Jan 1st (famous restaurants closed Jan 1-3), trains packed Dec 30-31 and Jan 2-3. Famous shrines (Meiji-jingu, Asakusa) with huge crowds for hatsumode. Premium hotel rates everywhere.',
      'jpcal.sakura.tokyo': 'Sakura Tokyo (estimate)', 'jpcal.sakura.tokyoMsg': 'Estimated hanami (cherry blossom) window in Tokyo, calibrated on 2020-2024. Ueno Park, Shinjuku Gyoen, Meguro River are the top spots. Crowded but magical. Exact dates are only known in February of the same year (JMA forecast).',
      'jpcal.sakura.kyoto': 'Sakura Kyoto (estimate)', 'jpcal.sakura.kyotoMsg': 'Estimated hanami window in Kyoto. Philosopher\'s Path, Maruyama Park, Daigo-ji. Peak bloom week — book ryokan/hotels well in advance.',
